# Historic American Buildings Survey (HABS), Historic American Engineering Record (HAER) documentation of Gettysburg National Military Park (National Park Service programmes; records and data PDFs held by the Library of Congress): first reading, workstream bt-web, 2026-11-16.
# How to read the page numbers: page 0 of each saved text is the Library's record (title, notes verbatim: the Library's own summary of the programme's significance statement and building dates); page n (n >= 1) is page n of the data PDF. The printed page after the slash is the page number the report prints on that page (the data PDFs of 1980 onward print one).
# Dating: a form or drawing set documents the building as it stood when it was measured (1930s to 2010s). A material or plan statement therefore refers to that survey date (forced indirect '~' or none '!') unless the record dates a part itself. A 'before 18xx' or 'ca. 18xx' date of initial construction is a bound on the first building (direct for existence in July 1863, hedged when the form says assumed or thought). A significance note that ends in an ellipsis is the Library's own abridgement of the programme text; only the printed part is quoted.
# The typed forms of the 1950s (HABS Nos. PA-342 to PA-363, Agnes A. Gilchrist, Architectural Historian, National Park Service) were looked at as page images (the dates and materials read from the images); where the text layer misreads a word the definition carries an explicit {{saved|fixed}} pair or a cleaned quote. The HAER, the Cyclorama Building report and the Trostle summer kitchen report are born-digital text and were not looked at as images.
# Hard rule kept: nothing here gives a profile of an 1863 wall, fence or building from a rebuilt, restored or rehabilitated state; such statements are marked indirect or none.

